+## Revisions
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+A visit is a chain of **revisions**. Each revision holds a complete set of scientific fields — never a diff — together with its author, the server receipt time, and the revisions it was edited from (its **bases**). A worker revision has one base, or none when it creates the visit. A resolution revision names every head it resolves as a base, which is what collapses them. Revision IDs are per-visit integers assigned by the server in receipt order, so a coordinator reading a visit's history sees an inspectable sequence rather than opaque handles.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+A revision is a **head** when no accepted revision names it as a base. A visit with exactly one head is **settled**, and that head is the visit's current record. A visit with two or more heads is **contested**: its heads are competing complete versions of the same visit, and the visit has no current record until a coordinator resolves them.
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+Original submissions are immutable. Resolution adds revisions; it never rewrites or deletes one.
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+## Submission
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+A submission carries the visit UUID, the plot UUID, the base revision ID (absent when creating the visit), an idempotency key, and the scientific fields. The author is taken from the authenticated session rather than the payload.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+Acceptance requires an authenticated worker, field types that validate, a plot UUID the catalog resolves, and — for an edit — a base revision ID that exists on that visit. A submission naming a base revision from a different visit, or one that does not exist, is rejected rather than stored as a new head; a tablet in that state has a stale or corrupt local record, and silently rooting a new head would create review work out of a client bug.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+A retry with an accepted idempotency key and an identical payload returns the original acceptance result. Reuse with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+An accepted edit whose base is a head extends that head: the chain advances and the head count is unchanged. An accepted edit whose base is any other revision adds a head, which makes the visit contested. The same rule applies to a submission against an already-contested visit — it is accepted and retained, extending one head or adding another. Submissions are never refused because a visit is under review, because a field worker's correction is not less valid for having arrived mid-resolution.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Retrieval
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+Retrieval of a visit returns its state — settled or contested — and never a single payload the caller could mistake for an agreed record. A settled visit returns its head as the current record. A contested visit returns all competing heads with their authors, receipt times, and bases, and no current record; a caller that needs one has to wait for resolution.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+History retrieval returns every revision of a visit with its author, receipt time, base, and whether it was authored by a field worker or by a resolution, so the path from any submitted observation to the current record is walkable.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+## Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves a contested visit by submitting a **resolution revision** that names every head it resolves and becomes the visit's single head. It takes one of two forms:
+
+- **Selection** — the coordinator names one head whose payload stands as the resolved visit. The resolution revision copies that payload and records which head was selected, so a caller reading the current record never has to dereference another revision.
+- **Composition** — the coordinator supplies a complete set of scientific fields, drawing on the competing heads as they judge. This is what unrelated edits need: when one worker corrected an observer name and another added a species, neither head is the right answer, and selection alone would make a coordinator choose which correction to lose.
+
+Both forms store the coordinator's identity, the resolved head IDs, and the form used. Composed fields validate exactly as a worker submission does.
+
+A resolution is rejected if the heads it names are not precisely the visit's current head set — a worker submission or another coordinator's resolution landed first. The rejection returns the current head set so the coordinator re-reads the visit and resolves what is actually contested, rather than overwriting an edit they never saw.
+
+## Public Contract
+
+| Operation | Request | Response |
+|---|---|---|
+| Submit visit revision | visit UUID, plot UUID, base revision ID (optional), idempotency key, scientific fields | accepted revision ID, resulting visit state |
+| Get visit | visit UUID | state; current record when settled, competing heads when contested |
+| Get visit history | visit UUID | all revisions with author, receipt time, base, origin; resolution records |
+| Resolve contested visit | visit UUID, resolved head IDs, form (selection with head ID, or composition with scientific fields) | resolution revision ID, resulting visit state |
+
+Visit states:
+
+- `settled` — one head; it is the current record and is eligible for export.
+- `contested` — two or more heads; unresolved review work exists and the visit has no current record.
+
+## Downstream Obligations
+
+Tablet-sync keeps the base revision ID of whatever revision a local draft was edited from and sends it with the submission; it presents a contested visit as competing versions awaiting coordinator resolution, not as an editable current record.
+
+Review-export presents a comparison of competing heads for coordinators, offering both resolution forms, and holds contested visits out of the Friday CSV — the export set is the settled visits.
+
+Both nodes own how they realize these obligations. Neither defines its own concurrency semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Unit of concurrency | Whole-visit revisions, each naming its base | Field-aware three-way merge over structured changes; last write wins | A merge rule that combines unrelated fields automatically cannot tell an unrelated pair from a scientifically related one, and would quietly combine a species identification with another worker's cover estimate. A larger review queue is the accepted cost of never combining observations without a coordinator looking. Last write wins discards submitted edits. |
+| Resolution forms | Selection and composition | Selection only | Unrelated concurrent corrections make every head wrong somewhere; selection alone would force a coordinator to drop one correction to resolve the visit. |
+| Submission to a contested visit | Accepted and retained as a head | Rejected until the visit is resolved | Rejecting makes a 48-hour-old field correction undeliverable because of review timing, and leaves the worker holding the only copy. |
+| Stale resolution | Rejected, returning the current head set | Resolve the heads named and leave later arrivals contested | Two coordinators, so simultaneous resolution is credible; returning the head set makes the coordinator read the edit they had not seen rather than resolve around it. |
+| Invalid base revision ID | Rejected | Stored as a new head | A base that does not exist means a stale or corrupt local record, not a concurrent edit; admitting it would manufacture review work from a client bug. |
+| Revision identity | Per-visit integers in receipt order | Content hashes; global sequence | Coordinators read revision history directly; a short per-visit sequence is legible in a comparison screen without tooling. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency is decided over whole visits; the scientific fields are opaque to the concurrency layer.
+2. ✅ A contested visit has no current record, rather than a provisional one.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether a submission may reference an archived plot UUID when correcting a visit recorded before the plot was archived.
+2. Whether a coordinator may resolve a settled visit — correcting a record no worker has contested.
+3. Retention of superseded revisions over multiple seasons; nothing is deleted in the first release.
